  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nBroad southwest flow is spreading into the Central Plains ahead of \nthe closed upper low entering the Intermountain West this afternoon.\nThe lee sfc trough continues to build upstream, evident by the \nstrong south winds across the area from 20 to 30 mph sustained, \ngusting up to 50 mph at times through the afternoon. A Wind Advisory \nwas issued for portions of north central Kansas where the strongest \nwinds were observed. Winds should ease slightly this evening and \novernight from 10 to 20 mph sustained while maintaining decent warm \nadvection for Saturday morning lows to only reach the lower 50s. \n\nLow to mid clouds increase Saturday as the primary upper shortwave \ntrough enters the Central Plains, driving a strong cold front \nsoutheastward in the afternoon and evening hours. Deterministic and \nCAMs show strong synoptic forcing and good low-level convergence \nalong the front for widespread showers (70-95%). Coverage of storms \nshould be more widely scattered given the weak elevated instability \n(< 500 J/KG) and marginal mid level lapse rates, despite the \nadequate bulk shear around 50 kts perpendicular to the boundary. \nForecast soundings exhibit strong capping near 800 MB, limiting the \npotential for widespread severe wind gusts to reach the sfc. Still \ncannot rule out a stronger updraft capable of severe wind gusts, \nespecially if temperatures can warm further than current forecast \nacross central Kansas. Brief, heavy rainfall could also impact travel\nwith saturated profilers and persistent lift behind the cold front \ninto the late evening period. CAMs have trended up with QPF amounts \nand have adjusted as such between 0.5 and 1.5 inches through Sunday \nmorning.\n\nStrong CAA overspreads the region, cooling temps to the \nlow 30s Sunday morning as dry weather returns through next week. \n12Z ensembles demonstrate good consensus on a colder, below-normal \ntemp pattern as a strong Canadian High settles into the region. NBM \nexhibits small spreads in temperatures, suggesting high confidence \nin highs in the low to middle 40s and overnight lows in the 20s each \nday through Thanksgiving.  A weak southern stream low passes south \nof the region on Tuesday-Wednesday, favoring precipitation over \nTexas and the Gulf coast where better moisture resides.",
